#2  What is sound?
===============================================================================================

  Sound is a variation of pressure propagating through matter.
  When this matter is in contact with our ears (usually air or water) and our brain notices it, it's called hearing.

  We've seen, with Fourier, that this phenomenon is always decomposable in an infinite sum of partials, called "spectrum".
  Each partial is a sinusoid. So everytime a variation of pressure propagates through matter, the phenomenon can be expressed as
  periodic oscillations -- infinite sum means all frequencies are always present if we admit that their amplitude can be zero.

  We don't necessary hear identical repeated patterns and yet that's all we ever hear. Unless their frequency is too low (infrasonic)
  or too high (ultrasonic).
  Random sounds are deterministic: digital white noise is generated by randomising each sample and yet it produces all frequencies,
  that is: oscillations of fixed period.
  Spinoza wrote (Ethics) that everything has a cause and that "random" only means "of ignored cause".
  Reject this principle and no science is left.

  If there's movement, there's energy. Energy takes many forms and is always convertible (although modern physics is currently stuck
  with entropy).
  Taking advantage of this convertibility is what analog circuits do: variations of pressure become variations of voltage.
  But the movement is reproduced, hence the name "analog" meaning equivalent in a different form.

  Therefore Fourier's decomposition applies to analog signals. Any voltage is a spectrum. If it's a pure DC offset (constant voltage),
  then all partials have an amplitude of zero except the one whose frequency is zero.
  But the integral is valid.
  We've seen (Chapter I) that the inverse Fourier transform is a special case of the inverse Laplace transform -- so not all Laplace is
  Fourier, but all Fourier is Laplace.
  Then the inverse Laplace transform can describe any analog signal.
  Whatever we feed our circuit, and whatever comes out, will oscillate at all frequencies (remember zero is a number).

  So if we focus on one isolated partial at the input, we know it will be present at the output. Phase and amplitude might not be the
  same (amplitude might be zero) but all frequencies are always everywhere.
  Even absolute silence is just all amplitudes equal to zero.
  That's right, silence is sound -- sometimes music even: ever listened to Franz Schubert?
  (In French, a crotchet rest is called a "soupir". It means "whisper".)
  A black picture is a picture. Silence is black sound.



