#5  H (s)
===============================================================================================

  Andante moderato.

  H (s) is the standard notation for what is called a TRANSFER FUNCTION. Sometimes G (s).
  Why H? Or G? Couldn't find any serious explanation.
  /*
    My undocumented gratuitous unnecessary uncalled for off-topic hypothesis:
      F is sometimes used for Laplace. It's also the initial of Fourier. Could be messy.
      Next in the Latin alphabet: G. But it's the initial of "gain". Ok, next: H.
  */
  Sometimes the variable is not s.
  Also it's often called H (jω) to make explicit that σ = 0.

  The transfer function of a system is a mathematical expression which binds any input to a deterministic output.
  
  https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Transfer_function        https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Determinism

  As we've shown: for LTI filters its value depends on the frequency.
  But for each partial it's a complex factor, constant over time, and equal to the ratio of the output over the input:

    Y (s) exp (st) / X (s) exp (st)  =  H (s)

  Back in Chapter I (#3), using Euler's formula, we've demonstrated that:
  "
    [...] any complex sinusoid in the form:

      ƒ (t)  =  X (s) exp (st)

    can be considered the positive-frequency-component of a real sinusoid with:

      Amplitude    α  =  2 |X (s)|

      Phase        φ  =  arg (X (s))  rad.
                                                        s
      Frequency    ω  =  s / j  rad./s    or    f  =  -----  Hz
                                                       2πj
  "

  Except it could be the negative component, depending on the value of s -- in which case  φ  =  -arg (X (s))  rad.
  It's ok, we were young and stupid back then.

  Also this was before we knew Laplace. So  s = jω  or  σ = 0.
  But this is also our hypothesis when feeding our RC filter with analytic signals.
  So what precedes stands.
  And the transfer function H (s) describes the system's response to signals of constant real amplitude and phase.
  Which explains why the equation involving a time-varying complex amplitude never provided a fully satisfying result.

  /*
    Actually let's try again.

      Y (s) exp (st)  =  H (s) X (s) exp (st)
      H (s) is a function of s = jω and ωc -- both constant over time for each partial.
      So if X (s) varies over time, H (s) is still a constant factor.

      Then (according to the chain rule):

          Y (s, t) exp (st)       dY
        d-------------------  =  ---- exp (st)  +  Y (s, t) s exp (st)
                 dt               dt

      and (according to the constant factor rule, then the chain rule):

          H (s) X (s, t) exp (st)              X (s, t) exp (st)             dX
        d-------------------------  =  H (s) d-------------------  =  H (s) ---- exp (st)  +  H (s) X (s, t) s exp (st)
                    dt                                dt                     dt
      
                                              dX
                                    =  H (s) ---- exp (st)  +  Y (s, t) s exp (st)
                                              dt
      So:

         dY                                              dX
        ---- exp (st)  +  Y (s, t) s exp (st)  =  H (s) ---- exp (st)  +  Y (s, t) s exp (st)
         dt                                              dt

      Dividing both sides by exp (st):

        dY/dt  +  Y (s, t) s  =  H (s) dX/dt  +  Y (s, t) s

      Subtracting  Y (s, t) s  from both sides:

        dY/dt  =  H (s) dX/dt

      The output's rate of change is proportional to the input's rate of change, by a factor which happens to be the transfer function.
      Simpler proof:

        Y (s)  =  H (s) X (s), so by the constant factor rule:  dY/dt  =  H (s) dX/dt

      but where's the fun in simple?
  */

  Ok back to business.
  So: real amplitude is twice the absolute value of the complex amplitude and phase is the argument of the complex amplitude.
  So if  y  =  Y (s) exp (st)  =  H (s) X (s) exp (st),
  its real amplitude is (Chapter I, #9):

    2 |Y (s)|  =  2 |H (s) X (s)|  =  |H (s)| * 2 |X (s)|

  In plain English -- and THIS IS FUNDAMENTAL -- the real amplitude of the output is the real amplitude of the input multiplied by the
  absolute value of the transfer function (|H (s)| or |H (jω)|).

  //  Example:

  ωc = 1  rad/s
  ω  = 2  rad/s
               ωc
  H (s)  =  --------
             s + ωc

  Then:                                                               ___________
                 |     ωc    |        |ωc|            √(ωc²)         ⎟    ωc²
    |H (jω)|  =  | --------- |  =  -----------  =  -------------  =  ⎟ ---------
                 |  jω + ωc  |      |jω + ωc|       √(ω² + ωc²)      ⎷  ω² + ωc²

  Some steps are useless right now. Except this shows that |H (jω)| is the square root of a ratio.
  Hence the notation:

    |H (jω)|²    which just means: "let's temporarily ignore the square root".

  Don't get confused if you stumble upon it: it's precisely meant to reduce distraction.

  So:  |H (jω|  =  √(ωc²) / √(ω² + ωc²)  =  ωc  /  √(ω² + ωc²)

  With  ωc = 1  and  ω = 2, that's:    |H (2j)|  =  1 / √(2² + 1²)  =  1 / √(4 + 1)  =  1 / √(5)

  So if    x  =  5 cos (2t)    then    y  =  5 cos (2t ± φ) / √(5)  =  √(5) cos (2t ± φ)


